  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-122325",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14108748/assets/8faba5a783797f7025f51ec1ae587cb68355dff7138278689346c2f713275562.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{-2x}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x} & =t+K \\\\ {e}^{2x} & =2t+C \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2t+1).\n$$",
